  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1104 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nAt the upper-levels, a deep low pressure system is digging down into \neastern Ontario, but it leaves very stagnant flow above the forecast \narea. Near to the surface, a weak high pressure system rests over \nnorthern Kansas seen clearly at 850 MB. Similar to last night, some \nvery thin and patchy fog is possible tonight with visibilities \ndropping down to 4-5 miles in fog prone areas. This will keep \nweather exceeding quiet and winds light. Thursday will continue to \nsee highs in the mid-80s and light winds owing to the high pressure.\n\nThe next few days continue to look exceedingly quiet with virtually \nno active weather. These highs in the 80s will continue through the \nweekend and remain within the pattern that is around 10 degrees \nwarmer than normal for this time of the year. By the weekend winds \nwill uptick considerably with winds of 20-30 out of the south. No \nweather headlines are forecast over this span even with the winds. \nThe relative humidities will stay well above fire weather concerns \nat 30-35%. \n\nSunday into Monday, ensembles have an upper-level trough developing \nover the West Coast. This pattern change will start heavily \nimpacting SW Kansas into Tuesday with Highs considerably cooler down \nin the 60s. A chance of widespread precipitation returns. Ensembles \nhave chances around 20-30%. The same ensembles only have amounts in \nthe 0.01-0.05\" range. Some uncertainty remains about the \nprecipitation, but based on that information early indications \nsuggest it may be widespread light rain. Clearer forecasts will \narrive in the coming days when the synoptic system is better \nresolved.",
